WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.699 --> 00:00:08.220
What do you imagine when I say ‘the future
of human evolution?’ A superbeing with powers

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:14.580
beyond comprehension? A mutated oddity far
removed from our idea of humanity? Or perhaps

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.640
nothing but decaying remains left in the wake
of our extinction? Scientists and sci-fi authors

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.300
have long speculated on what our future selves
will look like — and as technology advances,

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:32.009
our species might evolve much faster than
natural selection would typically allow. So,

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:37.430
for this entry into the archive, we’ll explore
posthuman scenarios from the absurd to the

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:45.260
frighteningly plausible — and meet the many
possible successors to humankind… Let’s

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.960
say hello to our first potential offspring.
I hope you’re excited… to meet Graham.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.640
Graham is a distant descendant of humanity
whose body has evolved to survive car crashes.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.850
Despite appearances, he isn’t a CG model,
but a life-sized statue made of silicon and

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:08.630
real human hair. Graham was created by artist
Patricia Piccinini, a sculptor known for her

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:13.840
hyper-realistic depictions of altered humanoids.
If you’ve seen clickbait on the internet

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.670
of human-animal hybrids, chances are the image
was actually one of her sculptures. Her work

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.520
can be disarming, unsettling — yet there’s
an unmistakable tenderness to each piece,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.720
a humanity that underscores her themes of
bioethics and future evolution. Unlike Piccinini’s

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.570
other statues, Graham was created as part
of a road safety campaign, and comes with

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:42.330
his own website that allows you to look under
his skin and study the anatomy beneath. Graham’s

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:48.070
fictional biology is shockingly detailed.
His skull has evolved crumple zones to absorb

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:53.930
impacts, his neck is encircled by ribs that
work like a brace, his torso is full of airbag-like

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:59.930
sacs to protect his vitals. It’s, uh…
I mean, it’s definitely memorable, as safety

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:05.680
campaigns go. Looking past the weirdness,
Graham does succeed in making you aware of

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:11.510
how vulnerable your body is — how unequipped
humans are for the dangers of modern life.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:16.840
In the U.S. alone, an estimated two million
people are injured annually in car accidents,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:22.090
and at least thirty thousand never make it
home at all. The ecosystem that humans have

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:27.599
built is so alien, so fundamentally different
from the natural world, it’s difficult to

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:32.550
say how evolution will react. It’s of course
highly unlikely we’ll ever look anything

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:38.390
like Graham, since evolution works on a very
different timescale than technological advancement.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:43.210
But humans are still evolving — our bones
have gotten lighter as we’ve transitioned

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:48.580
to a sedentary lifestyle. The average body
temperature is dropping due to improved sanitation

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:54.230
reducing infections. Even in our controlled
modern environments, evolution is an engine

00:02:54.230 --> 00:03:01.069
that can’t be turned off... …But perhaps
it could be sped up. Man After Man is a bizarre

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:05.970
artbook by Dougal Dixon that explores how
genetic engineering could create new branches

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:11.780
in the human family tree far sooner than evolution
could. Like our friend Graham, the work is

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:16.920
more of a surreal thought experiment than
something that holds together scientifically.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:23.180
Over eons of genome manipulation, some humans
become multi-armed oddities spliced from multiple

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:29.060
bodies. Others become enormous blobs engineered
to be a source of food. Still others become

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.530
genetically altered beasts of burden with
these giant air-conditioner heads that are…

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:41.060
practical, somehow? Some of these modified
humans later evolve further via natural selection,

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:47.099
taking on even wackier forms. There’s a…
peculiarity to the art which has made it famous

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:52.590
online. The project would even get a spiritual
successor in the form of C. M. Kosemen’s

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:58.890
All Tommorrows — a take on genetically engineered
posthumans which is, in my opinion, more cohesive

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:04.920
both aesthetically and thematically. Yet for
all their eccentricities, Man After Man and

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:10.520
Piccinini’s sculptures are unsettling meditations
on humanity’s uncertain future — reminding

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:16.340
us that change is inevitable, and not always
pretty. Evolution doesn’t necessarily mean

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:21.410
advancement, and depending on the choices
we make today, we may not recognize ourselves

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:29.740
tomorrow. Fascination with future human evolution
isn’t anything new. Stories about humanity’s

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.419
successors go all the way back to the nineteenth
century — with works like The Time Machine,

00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:40.830
by H.G. Wells, featuring humans separating
into a predator and prey species. A few decades

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:47.120
later, Olaf Stapledon’s novel Last and First
Men chronicled eighteen future human species

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:53.169
over two billion years. The book was adapted
into an experimental film in 2020 — which

00:04:53.169 --> 00:04:58.430
depicts vast, black and white landscapes of
crumbling monuments as a narrator reads from

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:04.680
sections of the novel. They speak of elephantine
humans with long, twisting trunks; bear-like

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:10.169
humans who dwell in the wild; and telepathic
superbeings who build an empire that lasts

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:16.360
for untold millennia. The scope of Last and
First Men can be overwhelming, even exhausting

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.449
— a relic from an era where the chief limit
to how long humanity had to evolve was the

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:27.199
eventual collapse of the sun. But soon, a
new invention would fundamentally alter our

00:05:27.199 --> 00:05:34.070
confidence in the future. Narrator: “A Nuclear
blast and the subsequent fallout will have

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:41.590
many adverse effects on life as we know it.
Intensive, prolonged radiation exposure may

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:48.039
transform those aboveground into decrepit,
rotting beings that have lost their ability

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:53.550
to reason.” Our vision of humanity’s future
often reflects humanity’s present, which

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:59.330
is why during the Atomic Age, fear of nuclear
devastation gave rise to a very unique type

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:05.419
of posthuman. Throughout the 50s and 60s,
sci-fi films about the far future depicted

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:11.520
the Nuclear Mutant – descendants of humanity
transmuted into inhuman brutes from exposure

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.650
to radiation. These post-apocalyptic humanoids
were often monstrous to the point of absurdity,

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:24.009
with radioactivity portrayed as an all-powerful
catalyst for evolutionary change. In the 1955

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:31.050
film The Day the World Ended, nuclear fallout
rapidly turns humans in, uh, whatever this

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:36.710
is. Yet beyond their silly appearance, Nuclear
Mutants gave a face to the existential threat

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:42.420
of atomic annihilation, turning abstract dangers
into something tangible that human survivors

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.560
could overcome. Narrator: “It is the unfortunate
truth that when you go above ground, you will

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:54.740
be faced with many post-nuclear nuisances.”
It’s notable that alongside mutants, most

00:06:54.740 --> 00:07:00.550
Atomic Age apocalypses had survivors — pure,
unaltered humans descended from those who

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:05.860
took shelter in bunkers before the bombs fell.
The trope echoes Cold War attitudes towards

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:10.710
preparedness — implying that with a little
good old fashion preparation, you and your

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:16.389
family won’t have to become mutants come
the end of the world. The 1956 movie World

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:21.870
Without End epitomizes this concept, presenting
an Earth cleanly split between an advanced

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:26.680
group of beautiful humans who had the foresight
to take shelter when the bombs fell, and a

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:32.360
horde of surface-dwelling mutants who were
caught in the blast. The idea that all of

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:36.930
humanity might change or die out, that no
amount of preparedness might be enough to

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:44.919
protect the status quo, is rarely confronted
in such stories. You don’t see many radiation-born

00:07:44.919 --> 00:07:50.400
mutants in modern media. There are exceptions
— especially in retro-future franchises

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.810
like Fallout — but that’s a series that
delights in recreating the tropes of Atomic

00:07:54.810 --> 00:08:01.669
Age sci-fi. Most modern depictions of nuclear
devastation are more subdued and somber — perhaps

00:08:01.669 --> 00:08:07.229
because real disasters like Chernobyl and
Fukushima have brought the reality of radiation’s

00:08:07.229 --> 00:08:12.020
long-term effects further into the world-wide
consciousness. Radiation doesn’t produce

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:18.860
campy monsters. Its effects aren’t charismatic
— most mutations, if they occur, are quiet

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:24.280
and tragic. And there’s no conquering it
— not really. You can react — evacuate

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:30.150
and quarantine the best you can, but ultimately,
it’s a force, like a car crash, that humans

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:35.899
simply haven’t been evolving to survive.
One option for humanity’s future, perhaps

00:08:35.899 --> 00:08:40.580
the first one that came to mind when you saw
the title of this video… is that we don’t

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:46.350
get one. That if the end of civilization comes,
it will be the type of ending that doesn’t

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:51.200
leave survivors. Nuclear mutants certainly
don’t seem like an optimistic vision for

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.720
future humanoids — but I think they are,
in a way. Because in order for there to be

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:01.360
mutants, there has to be something to mutate
— some form of humanity that endured into

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.400
the post-apocalypse. But what if we don’t
even get that? There’s an animated short

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:13.060
film from 1939 titled Peace on Earth that
depicts humanity’s total extinction with

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:19.079
shocking directness for its time. In the short,
the last humans perish on a grim battlefield

00:09:19.079 --> 00:09:24.589
that echoes the imagery of World War I. A
remake of the film titled Goodwill to Men

00:09:24.589 --> 00:09:31.890
was released in 1955, featuring an updated
atomic arsenal. At the end of both narratives,

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:36.850
after humanity is completely wiped out, a
group of cute cartoon animals crawl from the

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:42.060
wreckage and eventually take our place on
planet earth, evolving a society and building

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:48.260
a civilization from the helmets of fallen
soldiers. It’s so deeply bizarre, with a

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.959
huge amount of tonal dissonance — especially
since this was apparently shown to children.

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:59.170
Yet there’s an undeniable power to both
shorts, which convey the same theme of humanity’s

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:04.570
impermanence. Humans aren’t sacred, and
the world will continue to turn in our absence.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:09.380
And as freaky as many of the posthumans are
that we’ve explored so far, this ending

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:14.870
to humanity’s story — though simple and
understated — is probably the one that unsettles

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:20.360
me the most. Turning into a monstrous oddity
might not be an ideal future… but it seems

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:28.720
better than turning into a fossil. Human extinction
as our evolutionary future can be better understood

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:34.639
by looking into our past. In the game Ancestors:
The Humankind Odyssey, you play as a series

00:10:34.639 --> 00:10:40.720
of hominin species within the human evolutionary
lineage. And while not everything in Ancestors

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:46.730
is scientifically accurate, it is an exceptionally
unflinching look at the grinding slog that

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:52.850
is evolution. You die a lot in this game.
Like our ancestors, you are given almost no

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:57.500
instruction — the only way to figure out
which plants are poisonous and which animals

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:03.120
will try to eat you is through slow, agonizing
repetition. In the early stages your screen

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.220
will flash with images of gnashing teeth,
a representation of your primate brain being

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:13.590
overwhelmed by fear. If ever there’s been
a piece of media that captures our vulnerability

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:19.510
as a species and the randomness that we made
it at all, it’s this one… What the game

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:24.399
doesn’t capture — what it can’t capture
with its gameplay of continuous forward progress

00:11:24.399 --> 00:11:30.089
– is that evolution isn’t just a single
line. There were other branches in the hominin

00:11:30.089 --> 00:11:35.640
family tree — just three hundred thousand
years ago, as many as nine other species of

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.110
humans walked the earth alongside us, many
of whom may have been our intellectual equals.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:47.910
And now, well, now there’s only one. We
know extinction is possible for hominins,

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:53.829
because it’s happened before. Homo sapiens
aren’t the rule… we’re the exception.

00:11:53.829 --> 00:11:58.889
The concept of humans eventually fading out
like the other hominins is famously explored

00:11:58.889 --> 00:12:04.959
in the 1968 film Planet of the Apes. Based
on a novel by Pierre Boulle, the film begins

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:10.690
with three astronauts landing on a mysterious
planet that is dominated by a group of advanced

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:16.351
apes, who regard their planet’s human population
as nothing more than mindless animals. Through

00:12:16.351 --> 00:12:21.220
this inversion, the film investigates how
humankind has historically mistreated other

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:26.880
animals and each other. It’s a theme especially
highlighted by the ending, where — spoiler

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:31.070
alert for a film that’s over a half-century
old — it turns out the characters were on

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:40.680
Earth in the future the whole time. “Oh
my god, I was wrong - it was Earth all along.”

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:45.700
A question central to Planet of the Apes,
particularly the recent prequel films, is

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:51.110
whether or not humans are capable of peacefully
coexisting with another intelligent species.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:57.180
It’s a question that, tragically, may have
already been answered. We can’t definitely

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:02.450
prove the extent to which Homo sapiens were
responsible for the extinction of our fellow

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:07.209
hominins… but we do know that their disappearance
correlates very closely with the spread of

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:13.060
our ancestors. The ‘villain’ of the original
Planet of the Apes claims humans are a destructive

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:18.399
and territorial species, that violence is
intrinsic to human nature. And while one wants

00:13:18.399 --> 00:13:22.910
to push back, to point at how far we’ve
come and say ‘no, we are more than just

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:28.029
violent animals, we are special and we are
going to make it’ — it’s hard not to

00:13:28.029 --> 00:13:33.810
look at all we’ve done, the weapons we’ve
created, and wonder. Wonder if the last hominins

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:41.950
Homo sapiens will destroy… will be ourselves.
An equally harrowing vision of the future

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:47.440
is that humanity’s own evolutionary successors
will — in a bitter twist of fate — be

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:53.250
the ones to wipe us out. This patricide model
is prevalent in the wave of telekinetic sci-fi

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:59.550
films that surged in the 1970s and 80s. In
these narratives, humans born with telepathic

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:04.370
or other related abilities often come to see
themselves as the next phase in evolution

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:10.620
— and seek to eradicate or change the rest
of humanity. And the results are often cataclysmic,

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:17.160
with many films in this subgenre ending with
the ushering in of a new age. How these posthumans

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:21.750
are able to manipulate physics is a question
rarely answered with anything approaching

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:27.180
actual science. Probably the wildest explanation
comes from the more recent film Lucy, where

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:32.690
the main character develops the ability to
do… pretty much anything by using more than

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:37.560
10% of her brain. It’s an interesting film
visually, but as many of you are probably

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:43.860
aware, humans already use more than 10% of
our brains — we use the whole thing. But

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:48.490
while the science of these films is often
dubious, the premise of strife between humans

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:53.970
and posthumans seems far more plausible. In
the modern era, the idea of the superhuman

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:59.010
is probably most associated with the superhero
— who contrasts with other models of the

00:14:59.010 --> 00:15:05.269
posthuman in various ways. The typical superhero
is an expectational individual preoccupied

00:15:05.269 --> 00:15:11.440
with aiding a larger population of ordinary,
un-special humans. Rarely is there a fear

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.899
of replacement, because the superhero is an
outlier, unique — not here to usurp Homo

00:15:16.899 --> 00:15:23.301
sapiens but instead a benevolent superbeing
sworn to uphold the status quo. Exceptions

00:15:23.301 --> 00:15:29.180
to this model exist, with the most famous
probably being the mutants in the X-Men franchise.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:33.850
These characters are quite explicitly a new
form of hominin, and due to their frequent

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:39.930
marginalization, many mutants in the series
wish to avoid or outright replace humanity.

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:45.100
Scientifically, these mutants are, of course,
fairly implausible. If mutations actually

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.990
worked like this in nature, it would be impossible
to even go outside, because the animal kingdom

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:55.410
would be full of mice with laser eyes, or
fire-breathing tigers. Yet the theme of conflict

00:15:55.410 --> 00:16:00.779
between humanity and what comes next — both
in this franchise and across sci-fi — follows

00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:07.290
a precedent set by our own evolutionary history.
It is a fitting twist of fate that humanity’s

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:12.519
successors might treat us the same way we
treated our hominin relatives. If in a theoretical

00:16:12.519 --> 00:16:20.019
future superbeings came into existence, we’d
have to hope they weren’t like us... Yet

00:16:20.019 --> 00:16:25.589
there is a final alternate path that sci-fi
proposes — for humans to step outside the

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:31.310
flow of evolution altogether and advance to
a new phase of existence through technological

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:37.579
augmentation. Next to severe mutation or extinction,
casting off the weakness of flesh for the

00:16:37.579 --> 00:16:43.350
purity of metal might sound like an appealing
option. The idea of ascension via technology

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:49.769
has been around since the dawn of cinema.
The 1927 silent film Metropolis explores the

00:16:49.769 --> 00:16:55.060
concept of resurrecting a human in the form
of a machine over a decade before the first

00:16:55.060 --> 00:17:00.850
programmable computer. Mind uploading and
artificial immortality have long been cornerstones

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:06.370
of sci-fi — and are still widespread today.
It’s an alluring concept. Technology is

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:11.579
already changing what it means to be human.
If you’re someone who wears glasses or uses

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:17.880
the internet, you are, by some definitions,
already a semi-artificial posthuman — your

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:23.370
sense of self tied to a piece of technology.
Of course, both in sci-fi and the real world,

00:17:23.370 --> 00:17:28.780
not everyone has equal access to new tech,
which is a key reason most techno-futures

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:34.580
aren’t as glamorous as they first appear.
One of the most striking depictions of such

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:39.710
a future comes from the video game Signalis,
which stands as one of the more enigmatic

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:46.669
yet moving pieces of sci-fi in recent memory.
The game centers on posthumans called Replikas

00:17:46.669 --> 00:17:51.120
— human neural imprints downloaded into
the bodies of androids. It’s an imperfect

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:56.830
process — with the artificial minds suffering
breakdowns represented by sudden, bold flashes

00:17:56.830 --> 00:18:02.990
of text and shifting patterns. The gameplay
feels equally unstable, glitching from retro

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:08.820
survival-horror, to first person puzzles,
to long cutscenes — all the while overwhelming

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:14.230
you with these flashes of blurred text. If
the game Ancestors simulates having the mind

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:19.299
of an early hominin, Signalis simulates having
the mind of an artificial posthuman that is

00:18:19.299 --> 00:18:24.490
breaking down with equal forcefulness. At
one point the game overwhelms your senses

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:29.970
with a rapid-fire montage of classical oil
paintings. At another, just when a pivotal

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:35.270
action sequence is about to start, you instead
get text saying ‘violet scene missing,’

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:41.390
like your memory is short-circuiting. I’m
not going to pretend I understand everything

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:46.789
Signalis is getting at — frankly, I don’t
think anyone does. But I think it — like

00:18:46.789 --> 00:18:52.770
most works of techno-posthumanism, has a healthy
amount of skepticism when it comes to technological

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:58.400
ascension. The next phase of humanity may
very well be artificial, but no path into

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:05.990
the future is without serious potential drawbacks.
In the end, it seems that every option for

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:11.040
the future of human evolution — both natural
and artificial — could be its own type of

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:16.380
catastrophe. While making this video, I kept
expecting to find more optimistic visions

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:21.950
of humanity’s next stage, but for the most
part, across sci-fi, there are shockingly

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:28.549
few positive examples of what Homo sapiens
might evolve into. And maybe that’s a serious

00:19:28.549 --> 00:19:33.411
oversight. As something largely out of our
control, evolution can be frightening, but

00:19:33.411 --> 00:19:37.850
I think it’s important to remember that
change — while unavoidable — doesn’t

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:42.550
have to be negative. And if we ever want to
improve, we need to start imagining what a

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:49.260
better future could look like... As always,
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:54.200
please lend your support by liking, subscribing,
and hitting the notification icon to stay

00:19:54.200 --> 00:20:05.950
up to date on all things Curious. See you
in the next video.

